#
# Caddy reverse proxy for agrent.
#
# ── THIS FILE IS NOT DEPLOYED, AND IT IS NOT DRIFT-CHECKED ────────────────
#
# `deploy/apply.sh` copies ONLY docker-compose.vm.yml to the VM, and
# `deploy/check-drift.sh` hashes only that file. The Caddy container mounts
# `./Caddyfile` from /opt/agrent, and that copy is edited ON THE VM by hand.
# Editing this file changes nothing in production. It is the repo's record of
# what the live file should contain, and it is reconciled by hand in both
# directions — with nothing checking that the reconciliation happened.
#
# ── THE LIVE FILE, COMPARED ON THE VM 2026-09-10 (#842) ───────────────────
#
# /opt/agrent/Caddyfile was read on the VM — `gcloud compute ssh agrent
# --zone europe-west1-b`, which works with the configured account — and
# compared against this copy directive by directive. The live copy carries
# none of the comments above and indents with tabs; with comments stripped
# and whitespace normalised, the two files differ in exactly ONE token: the
# ACME contact in the global block. The live file carries the same
# `email {$ACME_EMAIL:...}` directive with a DIFFERENT default — the
# operator's PERSONAL mailbox.
#
# (An earlier revision of this header said the live file "was not reachable
# from where this was written" and asserted nothing on that basis. It is
# reachable. That claim is withdrawn too.)
#
# THAT ADDRESS IS DELIBERATELY NOT WRITTEN HERE, AND MUST NOT BE ADDED.
# This repository is PUBLIC, and the address appears in no tracked file
# today. Copying it in to make this copy "accurate" would publish an
# individual's personal email address into a public repo in order to settle
# a cosmetic mismatch in a directive that routes nothing but Let's Encrypt
# expiry notices — certificate issuance does not depend on it, and nothing
# about the running site is broken by the divergence.
#
# So this file keeps a ROLE address on purpose. `admin@agrent.bg` is the
# value the deployment SHOULD be using: it is safe in public, it survives a
# change of operator, and it is where an expiry notice ought to land.
#
# ── RECONCILING IT MEANS CHANGING THE VM, NOT THIS FILE ───────────────────
#
# Two ways, both VM-side:
#
#   1. Edit /opt/agrent/Caddyfile to the role address — back it up first
#      (`<file>.bak.<timestamp>`), then restart the caddy container.
#   2. Put ACME_EMAIL into the caddy container's environment.
#
# (2) is impossible as the stack stands. VERIFIED, in this repo, 2026-09-10:
# the `caddy` service in deploy/docker-compose.vm.yml declares NEITHER
# `env_file` NOR `environment` — unlike `app` and `worker`, which both carry
# `env_file` — so nothing here can put ACME_EMAIL into that container and the
# `{$ACME_EMAIL:admin@agrent.bg}` placeholder below always resolves to its
# default whenever this file is what runs. An earlier revision of this header
# claimed "it also sets ACME_EMAIL in the environment, overriding the default
# below"; that claim was not true of anything in the repo and has been
# withdrawn. Certificates already issued keep serving either way; the field
# decides who receives expiry mail, not whether TLS works.
#
# ── NOTHING IN THIS REPO DETECTS THIS DIVERGENCE ──────────────────────────
#
# The mismatch above was found by reading the VM by hand, and the next one
# will have to be too. `deploy/apply.sh` copies only docker-compose.vm.yml
# and `deploy/check-drift.sh` hashes only that file, so no check compares any
# Caddyfile against any other. Extending both to cover this file is the work
# that closes it; until then, treat this copy as a RECORD that was accurate
# on 2026-09-10 — for one deliberate exception — and not as a live mirror.
#
# `tests/guardrails/caddyfile-divergence.test.ts` holds the parts of the
# above a machine can check, including that every address in this file stays
# on the agrent.bg role domain.
#
# The site block serves the custom domain `app.agrent.bg` alongside the
# sslip.io host. Let's Encrypt issues a cert automatically on first request
# for each name in the site block.
#

{
    # Use the production LE endpoint by default (default behaviour).
    # ACME_EMAIL is NOT supplied by anything in this repo — the `caddy`
    # service has no `env_file` and no `environment` — so this resolves to
    # the default below. The live VM's copy defaults to a different,
    # personal address; the reconciliation is to change the VM, not to
    # paste that address into a public repo. See the header.
    email {$ACME_EMAIL:admin@agrent.bg}
}

35-187-80-26.sslip.io, app.agrent.bg {
    encode gzip zstd

    # Client-body limit roughly matches FILE_MAX_SIZE_BYTES above.
    request_body {
        max_size 110MB
    }

    # Security headers.
    header {
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
        X-Content-Type-Options    "nosniff"
        X-Frame-Options           "DENY"
        Referrer-Policy           "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        -Server
    }

    reverse_proxy app:3000 {
        # Next.js sits behind the proxy — forward the original host so
        # NextAuth + server-side redirects resolve to the public URL.
        header_up Host {host}
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
    }
}
